From 12eea3d387211114e7bcb5b0f62c19dba7b948ee Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 21:47:07 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] meta: make Facebook fetch the feed now instead of at 3am

Registering a schedule does not fetch anything. Commerce Manager showed the
Battle Ground data source minutes after a successful feed create as: type
"Manual upload", 0 products, status "No information found", last update
"Replaced every day". Registered, never run.

An empty catalog is not a cosmetic state. The next thing the product does with
it is build a product set, and Meta refuses outright:

  POST /1551227789809689/product_sets
  400  code 100  subcode 1798130  OAuthException
  "We disallow the creation of empty product sets. Please add products to your
   catalog and create a set that contains at least one product."
  trace AEXhoFufxIQpyY8Dovqw8It

Note the GET on the same edge succeeded first -- so this is not the permission
problem that came before it. The catalog is readable and writable. It is empty.

So a dealer connecting at 9am has a catalog that does nothing, a campaign that
cannot be built, and no explanation on screen, until the small hours.

POST /{product_feed_id}/uploads with the feed url forces the fetch immediately.
Fired on every successful provision, not just the first: re-running is how a
dealer repairs things, and "press it again and the inventory shows up" is what
that button already implies.

Best effort. The schedules are registered either way, so a failure here costs
freshness and not correctness, and failing a provision over an optimisation
would be worse than the wait it saves. It logs its own failure.

Also maps 1798130 to a dealer message that says what is actually happening.
It arrives as a bare code 100, classifies as 'request', and rendered as
"Facebook could not complete that request. Try again in a moment" -- wrong
twice, because nothing is broken and retrying in a moment does nothing.

tsc --noEmit --incremental false clean; npm run build clean.

